    <note xml:id="n1" type="philological" target="#s2w32" resp="#editor">
      The manuscript reading <quote>a Truckhouse</quote> reflects 18th-century colonial Atlantic usage (<mentioned>to truck</mentioned>, meaning to barter or trade), designating a government-regulated trading post where furs and pelts were exchanged at fixed tariff prices for European provisions and tools.
      <bibl corresp="#bibl_wicken_2002">Wicken, <title>Mi'kmaq Treaties on Trial</title>, ch. 6.</bibl>
    </note>
    <note xml:id="n2" type="historical" target="#s2w37" resp="#editor">
      On the phrase <quote>the River Chibenaccadie</quote>: <placeName ref="geonames:6147240">Shubenacadie</placeName> (from Mi'kmaw <mentioned>Sipekne'katik</mentioned>, "place where wild groundnuts grow") was a central Mi'kmaq district settlement along the Shubenacadie River, also named in the 1752 treaty with Jean-Baptiste Cope and interpreted in <title>R. v. Marshall</title> (1999).
      <bibl corresp="#bibl_marshall_1999"><title>R. v. Marshall</title>, [1999] 3 S.C.R. 456.</bibl>
    </note>
    <note xml:id="n3" type="intertextual" target="#s3" resp="#editor">
      Under the closing attestation <quote>Cha. Lawrence / Paul Laurent</quote>, the hunting, fishing, and truckhouse boilerplate in Paul Laurent's 10 March 1760 LaHave treaty recurs nearly verbatim across the 1760–1761 Halifax district exemplars (Shubenacadie, Richibucto, Miramichi), alongside each chief's distinctive clan totem mark.
      <bibl corresp="#bibl_nsa_1760">Nova Scotia Archives, RG 1, vol. 430, nos. 2–9.</bibl>
    </note>
